{"type":"message","id":"25015ed4","parentId":"d19db512","timestamp":"2026-05-21T07:36:51.226Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_wanxSaJ0XbpDmm0M1B9LzUYW","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"aave-defi-united-kelpdao-exploit-2026-04-28\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"Aave-Led DeFi United Recovery After the $293M Kelp DAO Exploit\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"Kelp DAO recovery\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"Aave Asks Arbitrum to Unfreeze Stolen Kelp DAO Funds to Contain Fallout\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Ethereum\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"ethereum\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"Cointelegraph\",\n    \"Decrypt\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/news/aave-linked-defi-united-outlines-plan-to-restore-rseth-backing-after-293m-exploit\",\n    \"https://decrypt.co/365724/aave-led-defi-united-relief-effort-raises-300-million-to-cover-kelp-dao-exploit-losses\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"Aave Labs asked the Arbitrum DAO to unfreeze 30,765 ETH, worth about $73.5 million, that was tied to the Kelp DAO attack.\",\n    \"The requested funds were meant to go to 'DeFi United', a recovery effort created to restore rsETH backing and compensate affected holders after the exploit.\",\n    \"The Kelp exploit was described as roughly a $292 million to $293 million incident.\",\n    \"Aave said the attacker used stolen rsETH as collateral on its lending platform, which left Aave with more than $190 million in bad debt and triggered panic withdrawals.\",\n    \"Aave's total value locked reportedly dropped nearly $12 billion in one week after the exploit.\",\n    \"The DeFi United effort had already attracted about $21 million in contributions, while another $215 million had been conditionally pledged by major protocols such as Arbitrum, Mantle, Ether.fi, and Lido.\",\n    \"Aave founder Stani Kulechov pledged 5,000 ETH of his own money to the recovery effort.\",\n    \"Aave Labs said the remediation process was expected to take roughly seven weeks, or about 49 days, and the funds would be returned if the recovery plan failed.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"Aave Labs has proposed that the decentralized autonomous organization behind Arbitrum unfreeze $73.5 million in Ether, or 30,765 ETH, tied to the Kelp DAO attack and direct those funds to 'DeFi United.' DeFi United is a recovery fund aimed at restoring the backing of Kelp DAO restaked ETH, known as rsETH, and compensating affected holders. The proposal came after the Arbitrum Security Council had frozen the 30,765 ETH in a wallet connected to the large Kelp exploit.\\n\\nThe Cointelegraph report framed the proposal as a plan to restore rsETH backing after a $293 million exploit, while the Decrypt report described the same effort as an Aave-led relief push to cover Kelp DAO losses. Both sources point to the same underlying crisis: a large exploit in which the attacker was able to create bad debt and destabilize parts of the DeFi ecosystem. The proposal was posted on Arbitrum's governance forum and argued that using the seized funds for remediation would amount to a meaningful contribution toward restoring normal conditions for Arbitrum users and the wider crypto market. Support for the submission was linked to Kelp DAO, LayerZero, Ether.fi, and Compound.\\n\\nAccording to the reporting, Aave was hit especially hard because the attacker used stolen rsETH tokens as collateral on Aave's lending platform to borrow wrapped Ether. That sequence left Aave with more than $190 million in bad debt and sparked panic withdrawals. The impact was not limited to Aave alone. Decrypt said Aave's total value locked dropped nearly $12 billion in just one week after the exploit. The scale of the event made the recovery effort feel urgent, and the recovery coalition was assembled quickly.\\n\\nThe DeFi United effort had already gathered about $21 million in active contributions at the time of the reporting. Those early contributions came from participants including Aave Labs head of contracts Emilio Frangella, Kelp DAO, Golem Foundation, BGD Labs, and the Bitcoin-native DeFi protocol Babylon. Aave founder Stani Kulechov also pledged 5,000 ETH of his own money to the push. Beyond those initial funds, another $215 million had been provisionally pledged by major protocols including Arbitrum, Mantle, Ether.fi, and Lido, although those contributions still depended on governance approvals. Additional protocols such as LayerZero, Ethena, Ink Foundation, and Frax Finance signaled that they wanted to help as well.\\n\\nThe recovery plan described the requested ETH as being sent to a recovery multisig controlled by Aave, Kelp DAO, and the blockchain security platform Certora. Aave Labs said that even a partial recovery would reduce the shortfall in a meaningful way. The group also laid out a timeline of roughly seven weeks, or about 49 days, for the process of unfreezing funds, restoring rsETH, and compensating holders. If the recovery effort failed for any reason, Aave said it would return the unthawed funds.\\n\\nThe broader context matters because the exploit was not just a protocol issue; it became a systemic DeFi coordination problem. The Cointelegraph report emphasized the goal of restoring rsETH backing, while Decrypt highlighted the relief effort's fundraising momentum and the breadth of support from major DeFi players. Taken together, the two reports show an ecosystem trying to ring-fence losses, repair trust, and prevent the attack from producing longer-term damage across lending markets and liquid staking positions.\\n\\nThe reporting also underlines how quickly the incident escalated into a governance and treasury response. Rather than focusing only on compensation after the fact, the proposal tried to use frozen assets from the exploit itself as part of the remedy. That approach made the Arbitrum DAO a key decision-maker in whether the recovery plan could go forward.\",\n  \"tweet_quotes\": []\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779349011226}}
